            0.063
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.088,
            0.67,
            0.107
          ],
          "content": "Let us compare the ratios for the sample table above."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.107,
            0.848,
            0.145
          ],
          "content": "The ratio of the volume of a sachet to a small bottle is 6 : 180. The ratio of their prices is 2 : 154. Are these ratios proportional?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.147,
            0.801,
            0.186
          ],
          "content": "Why do you think that the ratio of the prices is not proportional to the ratio of the volumes?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.189,
            0.801,
            0.264
          ],
          "content": "Discuss the pros and cons of different size bottles for the company and for customers. For reducing ecological footprint, what would you recommend to the company and to the customer? Does the same occur for other products?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.816,
            0.186,
            0.905,
            0.239
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_13_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.263,
            0.801,
            0.318
          ],
          "content": "Make similar tables for other products in the market, capturing different prices for different measures of the same product, e.g., rice or atta (flour)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.318,
            0.801,
            0.356
          ],
          "content": "Observe the products for which the prices are proportional to the different measures."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.356,
            0.803,
            0.394
          ],
          "content": "Discuss in class the proportionality of prices to measures of the same product."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.413,
            0.837,
            0.511
          ],
          "content": "**Note to the Teacher:** Give a project to students. Start by dividing the class into groups. Each group should go to one shop and collect prices for different measures of the same product. For example, they should note down the prices of 500 g of rice, 1 kg of rice, and 10 kg of rice. They should make tables with measure sizes and prices, and present them to the rest of the class. They should discuss if the prices are proportional or not, and why."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.539,
            0.551,
            0.565
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "7.5 Sharing, but Not Equally!"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.584,
            0.503,
            0.68
          ],
          "content": "**Activity 3:** Form a pair. Collect 12 countable objects or counters (it can be coins, seeds, or pebbles). Now, share them between the two of you in different ways."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.686,
            0.503,
            0.745
          ],
          "content": "If you divide them equally, what is the ratio of the number of counters with each of you?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.752,
            0.502,
            0.809
          ],
          "content": "Each of you will get 6 counters. So, the ratio is 6 : 6, or 1 : 1 in its simplest form."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.586,
            0.586,
            0.866,
            0.768
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_13_image_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.811,
            0.437,
            0.832
          ],
          "content": "Now let us not share equally."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.837,
            0.503,
            0.896
          ],
          "content": "If your partner gets 5 counters, how many objects will you get? What is the ratio of the counters?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.505,
            0.778,
            0.957,
            0.867
          ],
